1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,147
Lesson 11.4.

2
00:00:02,527 --> 00:00:05,320
Simulators and agentic research workflows.

3
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:08,925
So far we have written
the programs ourselves.

4
00:00:09,305 --> 00:00:13,995
The next idea is for an agent, a
language model with tools, to propose

5
00:00:13,995 --> 00:00:18,820
experiments, run them on a simulator,
read the results and improve them.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,222
That requires more than a
simulator: each experiment must

7
00:00:23,222 --> 00:00:26,961
be turned into a task a
machine can grade on its own.

8
00:00:27,341 --> 00:00:28,976
Today we build that piece.

9
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:32,396
The simulation layer has three levels.

10
00:00:32,776 --> 00:00:36,444
THRML simulates block sampling on GPUs.

11
00:00:36,824 --> 00:00:39,633
Torx describes and differentiates
stochastic programs.

12
00:00:40,013 --> 00:00:43,629
And Extropic offers a simulation
interface in early access,

13
00:00:43,629 --> 00:00:47,428
meant for agents to search for
algorithms that compile and run

14
00:00:47,428 --> 00:00:50,736
well on its hardware; its
documentation is not public,

15
00:00:51,116 --> 00:00:53,756
so here we work with the open libraries.

16
00:00:54,136 --> 00:00:59,510
What matters is that the agent only
sees an interface: code in, metrics out.

17
00:01:00,617 --> 00:01:03,193
A verifiable task has four parts.

18
00:01:03,573 --> 00:01:06,725
A statement: estimate
the magnetizations of a

19
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:09,947
four-by-four Ising model
with a block sampler.

20
00:01:10,327 --> 00:01:13,193
An isolated execution:
the agent's code runs

21
00:01:13,193 --> 00:01:15,865
in a separate process, with a time limit.

22
00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:22,072
A reference metric: maximum error
of 0.03 against exact enumeration.

23
00:01:22,452 --> 00:01:26,569
And a rubric: that it uses a sampler
and that the coloring is valid.

24
00:01:26,948 --> 00:01:29,628
The reward combines execution and rubric.

25
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:35,403
A scripted proposer, standing in for
the agent, submits five solutions.

26
00:01:35,783 --> 00:01:38,574
The executor gives fifteen
hundredths for running

27
00:01:38,574 --> 00:01:41,023
and eighty-five for
reproducing the metric.

28
00:01:41,403 --> 00:01:44,742
Chromatic Gibbs with
thirty sweeps: reward one.

29
00:01:45,122 --> 00:01:48,753
With three sweeps it runs
but does not converge: 0.4.

30
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:51,123
A syntax error: zero.

31
00:01:51,503 --> 00:01:56,193
And a cheat, which computes the
answer by exact enumeration: it passes

32
00:01:56,193 --> 00:02:01,084
execution with full marks, but the
rubric catches it, and it ends at 0.7.

33
00:02:02,217 --> 00:02:04,608
The fifth proposal gives
the most useful lesson.

34
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:09,516
Updating all spins at once is a
classic mistake: it is not Gibbs.

35
00:02:09,896 --> 00:02:14,095
And yet it passes the metric,
with an error of 0.019.

36
00:02:14,475 --> 00:02:15,007
Why?

37
00:02:15,387 --> 00:02:19,601
On a bipartite graph, simultaneous
updating is equivalent to two

38
00:02:19,601 --> 00:02:24,277
interleaved chains, each correct, so
the magnetizations come out right.

39
00:02:24,657 --> 00:02:28,110
But the correlations
between neighbors have an

40
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:31,713
error of 0.8, against
0.04 with chromatic Gibbs.

41
00:02:32,093 --> 00:02:35,897
The metric was under-specified;
only the rubric noticed.

42
00:02:37,017 --> 00:02:38,900
Design rules follow from this.

43
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,564
Measure everything that defines
the result, not just the

44
00:02:42,564 --> 00:02:45,321
easiest thing: first-
and second-order moments.

45
00:02:45,701 --> 00:02:48,671
Run the code in an
isolated environment with a

46
00:02:48,671 --> 00:02:51,318
time limit, because it is untrusted code.

47
00:02:51,698 --> 00:02:54,775
Give more weight to execution,
which is harder to fool,

48
00:02:54,775 --> 00:02:57,628
and use the rubric for what
execution does not see.

49
00:02:58,008 --> 00:03:00,569
And keep every version
of the environment: each

50
00:03:00,569 --> 00:03:03,349
change in the reward changes
what the agent learns.

51
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:09,305
In practice, package earlier labs as
tasks with reference metrics, grade

52
00:03:09,305 --> 00:03:14,565
execution with fifteen hundredths for
running and eighty-five for reproducing,

53
00:03:14,945 --> 00:03:18,189
and add a rubric with rules
or with a judge model.

54
00:03:18,569 --> 00:03:21,930
In lab thirty you will
build a small benchmark.

55
00:03:23,050 --> 00:03:26,782
Common mistakes: checking
only the easiest statistic.

56
00:03:27,162 --> 00:03:29,928
Running untrusted code without isolation.

57
00:03:30,308 --> 00:03:32,751
Rewarding the answer
instead of the method.

58
00:03:33,131 --> 00:03:35,879
And changing the reward without
versioning the environment.

59
00:03:36,983 --> 00:03:42,616
In summary, a research agent needs a
simulator and, above all, verifiable

60
00:03:42,616 --> 00:03:47,708
tasks: statement, isolated execution,
reference metric and rubric.

61
00:03:48,088 --> 00:03:51,119
In our harness, the rubric
caught both a cheat and

62
00:03:51,119 --> 00:03:53,906
an incorrect algorithm
that passed the metric.

63
00:03:54,286 --> 00:03:57,688
Designing the reward is designing
what the agent will learn.

64
00:03:58,817 --> 00:04:03,532
To practice: compute the reward of a
solution that runs without reproducing.

65
00:04:03,912 --> 00:04:08,029
Explain why simultaneous updating
gets the magnetizations right.

66
00:04:08,409 --> 00:04:10,608
Propose the metric that
would have caught it.

67
00:04:10,988 --> 00:04:13,314
And explain why execution
must be isolated.
